<article xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Sci Rep</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Sci Rep</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Scientific Reports</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2045-2322</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group UK</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">39747553</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">11696795</article-id>
<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">84273</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41598-024-84273-8</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Relationship between proximal to distal phalangeal articular angle and Hallux Pronation in Hallux Valgus deformity</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>Yun Jae</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>Ho-Seong</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>Young Rak</given-names></name><address><email>jeanguy@hanmail.net</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/02c2f8975</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.267370.7</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 0533 4667</institution-id><institution>Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Asan Medical Center, </institution><institution>University of Ulsan College of Medicine, </institution></institution-wrap>88 Olympic-ro 43-gil, Songpa-gu, Seoul, 05505 Republic of Korea </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>2</day><month>1</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>2</day><month>1</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><elocation-id>92</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>22</day><month>10</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>23</day><month>12</month><year>2024</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2024</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2024</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p><bold>Open Access</bold> This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="Abs1"><p id="Par1">This study aimed to determine whether hallux pronation influences the proximal to distal phalangeal articular angle (PDPAA) and evaluate its relationship with postoperative recurrence. We included 94 hallux valgus (HV) patients who underwent distal chevron metatarsal osteotomy (DCMO) alone (DCMO-only group, <italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;15) and DCMO with Akin osteotomy (DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin group, <italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;79). Preoperative additional toe radiographs were taken under supination stress to position the pronated toe as a true anteroposterior orientation. The mean follow-up period was 12.9 months. The PDPAA on weightbearing radiograph did not significantly differ from the PDPAA corrected by supination stress (5.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.0 vs. 4.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4 degrees, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.820). The DCMO-only group showed a higher recurrence rate than the DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin group (33.3 vs. 7.6%, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.014). In the DCMO-only group, those with recurrence had significantly higher preoperative PDPAA values than those without recurrence (7.6&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;5.9 vs. -0.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.0 degrees; respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.013). In conclusion, the PDPAA was not significantly influenced by the hallux pronation in patients with HV. For HV patients with a large preoperative PDPAA, additional Akin osteotomy should be considered to prevent recurrence.</p></abstract><kwd-group xml:lang="en"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Akin osteotomy</kwd><kwd>Hallux pronation</kwd><kwd>Hallux valgus recurrence</kwd><kwd>Hallux valgus interphalangeus</kwd><kwd>Stress radiograph</kwd></kwd-group><kwd-group kwd-group-type="npg-subject"><title>Subject terms</title><kwd>Outcomes research</kwd><kwd>Bone</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Springer Nature Limited 2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="Sec1"><title>Introduction</title><p id="Par2">In hallux valgus (HV) surgery, Akin osteotomy for correcting hallux valgus interphalangeus (HVI) is typically based on the residual deformity after correcting the HV deformity, and it has been reported that this decision cannot be reliably predicted preoperatively<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref></sup>. This is because HV is a multiplanar deformity with a pronation component, which makes accurate assessment of the HVI preoperatively difficult<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref></sup>. Several studies have noted an increase in the hallux valgus interphalangeus angle (HVIA) or the identification of previously unrecognized HVI following HV deformity correction<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref></sup>. These studies that evaluated HVI based on HVIA has suggested that the Akin osteotomy cannot be predicted preoperatively<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref></sup>. This raises the question of whether other parameters, besides HVIA, could be used to predict the need for Akin osteotomy before surgery.</p><p id="Par3">In this context, Kaufmann et al. explored whether the indication for Akin osteotomy could be determined using the proximal to distal phalangeal articular angle (PDPAA) measured on preoperative weight-bearing radiographs<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>. They reported that the postoperative hallux valgus angle (HVA) is maintained substantially better when concomitant Akin osteotomy was performed in patients with preoperative PDPAA exceeding 8 degrees than patients with PDPAA less than 8 degrees<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>. Also, PDPAA is reported to be less influenced by metatarsal pronation<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref></sup>. Lutz et al. showed that PDPAA is not related to the degree of metatarsal pronation classified as a round sign<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref></sup>. However, they also noted that a potential limitation exists in that the articular surface involved in measuring PDPAA is not perpendicular to the long axis of the proximal phalanx, which theoretically suggests that PDPAA could be affected by proximal phalanx rotation<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par4">In this study, we clinically evaluated the relationship between the pronation of the proximal phalanx and PDPAA in HV patients. We also assessed the association between preoperative PDPAA, postoperative recurrence, and the need for Akin osteotomy.</p></sec><sec id="Sec2"><title>Methods</title><sec id="Sec3"><title>Patient selection</title><p id="Par5">We employed a retrospective cohort study design. This study was performed in line with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Asan Medical Center (2023&#x02009;&#x02212;&#x02009;0421), and written informed consent was waived due to the retrospective nature of this study and the analysis used anonymous clinical data. No funds, grants, or other support was received for conducting this study.</p><p id="Par6">From June 2021 and March 2023, patients who underwent distal chevron metatarsal osteotomy (DCMO), with or without an Akin osteotomy for the treatment of hallux valgus at our single center were included in the study. All patients met the diagnostic criteria of a hallux valgus deformity with a preoperative HVA of more than 15 degrees or an intermetatarsal angle (IMA) of more than 10 degrees. Patients with a history of rheumatoid arthritis diagnosis and radiographic evidence of osteoarthritic change in the first metatarsophalangeal joint were excluded. We also excluded revision surgeries and cases with the follow-up period of less than 3 months. The minimum follow-up period was determined to be 3 months as previous data indicated that remarkable changes in HVA primarily occur during the first 3 months after surgery and stabilize thereafter<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref></sup>. A case with only Akin osteotomy was excluded because it was out of our focus. Cases with other metatarsal osteotomies (proximal or midshaft osteotomy) or lesser toe procedures (e.g., Weil osteotomy or resection arthroplasty of the second toe) were also excluded. Furthermore, cases with additional procedures unrelated to hallux valgus (e.g., flat foot surgery) in conjunction with HV surgery were excluded. From June 2021 to March 2023, 147 feet with total HV surgery were screened. Ultimately, 94 feet were eligible for the study: 15 cases (16%) in the DCMO-only group and 79 cases (84%) in the DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin group. (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>)</p><p id="Par7">
<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Flowchart diagram of subject recruitment.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_84273_Fig1_HTML" id="d33e247"/></fig>
</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec4"><title>Surgical technique</title><p id="Par8">A lateral release was first conducted using a first web dorsal incision. This included adductor tenotomy and metatarso-sesamoid capsular release as part of the lateral soft tissue procedures. The adequacy of the lateral release was evaluated using the varus stress test on the 1st metatarsophalangeal joint (MTPJ), with an optimal target of 20 degrees of varus shift. A bunionectomy was performed parallel to the first metatarsal shaft using an oscillating microsaw. The DCMO was then performed via a medial approach centered at the first metatarsal head. A 60 to 80 degrees angled chevron osteotomy was performed at the center of the first metatarsal head. The metatarsal head was laterally translated by 5 to 8&#x000a0;mm. Fixation of the displaced fragment was achieved with two 1.1-mm Kirschner wires inserted 2&#x000a0;cm proximal to the osteotomy site, and the remaining protruding bone was removed.</p><p id="Par9">An Akin osteotomy was performed based on the intraoperative reassessment of hallux valgus deformity after the soft tissue procedure and metatarsal osteotomy. The osteotomy was performed 7&#x000a0;mm distally from the base of the proximal phalanx and involved a 2 to 4&#x000a0;mm medial closing wedge osteotomy, aligned parallel to the MTPJ. Fixation was achieved using two 1.1-mm Kirschner wires buried under the skin.</p></sec><sec id="Sec5"><title>Radiographic evaluation</title><p id="Par10">We took anteroposterior (AP), lateral, and oblique radiographs of the foot and first toe in a standardized manner. All radiographs were taken under full weightbearing. Postoperative radiographs were taken between 2 and 4 weeks after surgery, with full weightbearing. From June 2021, we adopted the protocol for all hallux valgus patients undergoing surgery, which included taking an additional AP radiograph of the first toe with supination stress preoperatively. (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). These radiographs were obtained by the medical staff (Y.J.K), and supination stress was applied by holding the interphalangeal joint to position the first toe in true AP orientation (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). We defined the true AP position of the first toe as the orientation where the toenail is horizontally aligned with the ground when viewed from the front (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). We acquired radiographs for analysis taken at the preoperative, postoperative, 3-month follow-up, and the most recent follow-up stages. Two orthopedic surgeons, who had no involvement in the surgeries, independently evaluated all radiographs. For each parameter, the mean values from the two observers were used in the analysis.</p><p id="Par11">
<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Clinical photography and radiograph of the foot in a standard weight-bearing position and the toe in a true anteroposterior position under supination stress. <bold>(A)</bold> CC clinical photography of the foot under the standard weight-bearing position. <bold>(B)</bold> CC clinical photography of the foot in the true AP position of first toe corrected by supination. <bold>(C)</bold> AP clinical photography of the foot under the standard weight-bearing position. <bold>(D)</bold> AP clinical photography of the foot in the true AP position of first toe corrected by supination. <bold>(E)</bold> AP radiograph of the foot under the standard weight-bearing position. <bold>(F)</bold> AP radiograph of the first toe in the true AP position corrected by supination. CC: craniocaudal; AP: anteroposterior.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_84273_Fig2_HTML" id="d33e296"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par12">In AP radiographs, we measured the following parameters (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>) based on methods outlined in previous studies<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR12">12</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref></sup>. HVA was defined as the angle between the longitudinal axes of the first metatarsal bone and the proximal phalanx. IMA referred to the angle formed between the axes of the first and second metatarsal bones. PDPAA was defined as the angle between the two articular surfaces of the proximal phalanx of the great toe. A positive value indicated valgus alignment. HVA was used to assess the maintenance of hallux valgus correction during follow-up<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref></sup>. An HVA greater than 15 degrees during follow-up was defined as recurrence.</p><p id="Par13">
<fig id="Fig3"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Radiologic parameters on a foot AP radiograph. IMA: intermetatarsal angle; HVA: hallux valgus angle; PDPAA: proximal-to-distal phalangeal articular angle.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_84273_Fig3_HTML" id="d33e322"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par14">We investigated the impact of the hallux pronation deformity on the PDPAA by comparing PDPAA measurements from standard weightbearing radiographs to those taken with supination stress applied. Additionally, we compared radiographic outcomes between patients who underwent DCMO alone and those who had both DCMO and Akin osteotomy. Preoperative PDPAA values were also compared between patients who experienced recurrence and those who did not.</p><sec id="Sec6"><title>Statistical analysis</title><p id="Par15">Continuous variables were presented as mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;standard deviation, while categorical variables were expressed as percentages. The Shapiro-Wilk test was used to assess the normality of the data. For continuous variables, comparisons were made using either the Student&#x02019;s t-test or the Mann&#x02013;Whitney U test, depending on data distribution. Categorical data were analyzed using Pearson&#x02019;s chi-squared test or Fisher&#x02019;s exact test. All p-values were two-tailed, with statistical significance defined as <italic>P</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05. Statistical analysis was conducted using SPSS Statistics version 26.0 (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA).</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec7"><title>Results</title><p id="Par16">Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab1" ref-type="table">1</xref> shows the baseline demographics. The mean follow-up duration was 12.9 months (range; 3&#x02013;29 months), and the mean age at surgery was 60.2 years (range; 24&#x02013;78 years). The majority of patients were female, accounting for 93.6% of the total cases. There were no significant differences in baseline radiographic parameters between the DCMO-only group and the DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin group.</p><p id="Par17">
<table-wrap id="Tab1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Comparison of baseline characteristics between the DCMO-only and DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin groups.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Characteristics</th><th align="left">Total</th><th align="left">DCMO-only</th><th align="left">DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin</th><th align="left"><italic>P</italic> value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Case number</td><td align="left">94</td><td align="left">15 (16%)</td><td align="left">79 (84%)</td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left">Age, years</td><td align="left">60.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;13.3</td><td align="left">55.4&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;12.4</td><td align="left">61.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;13.4</td><td char="." align="char">0.118</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Female sex</td><td align="left">88 (93.6%)</td><td align="left">14 (93.3%)</td><td align="left">74 (93.7%)</td><td char="." align="char">1.000</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Right</td><td align="left">53 (56.4%)</td><td align="left">11 (73.3%)</td><td align="left">42 (53.2%)</td><td char="." align="char">0.169</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Left</td><td align="left">41 (43.6%)</td><td align="left">4 (26.7%)</td><td align="left">37 (46.8%)</td><td char="." align="char">0.169</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Follow-up duration, months</td><td align="left">12.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.2</td><td align="left">12.5&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.3</td><td align="left">13.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.3</td><td char="." align="char">0.831</td></tr><tr><td align="left">IMA, degrees</td><td align="left">14.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.5</td><td align="left">14.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.8</td><td align="left">14.8&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.5</td><td char="." align="char">0.423</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HVA, degrees</td><td align="left">33.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.9</td><td align="left">28.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;9.8</td><td align="left">33.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.5</td><td char="." align="char">0.055</td></tr><tr><td align="left">PDPAA, degrees (standing)</td><td align="left">5.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.0</td><td align="left">2.4&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;5.5</td><td align="left">5.4&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.2</td><td char="." align="char">0.125</td></tr><tr><td align="left">PDPAA, degrees (supination)</td><td align="left">4.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4</td><td align="left">3.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.5</td><td align="left">5.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4</td><td char="." align="char">0.316</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Data are presented as mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;standard deviation or number (%), unless otherwise indicated.</p><p>DCMO, distal chevron metatarsal osteotomy; HVA, hallux valgus angle; IMA, intermetatarsal angle; PDPAA, proximal-to-distal phalangeal articular angle.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par18">When preoperative radiographs were analyzed for all patients, the PDPAA on standard weightbearing views showed no significant difference compared to those taken with supination stress (5.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.0 vs. 4.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4 degrees, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.820).</p><p id="Par19">Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>; Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref> shows the radiographic outcomes. Postoperatively, the HVA significantly decreased in both groups. However, a slight increasing trend in HVA was observed in both groups by the 3-month follow-up. Recurrence occurred in 11 cases (11.7%), with 5 (33.3%) cases in the DCMO-only group and 6 (7.6%) cases in the DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin group at the final follow-up.</p><p id="Par20">
<table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Radiographic parameters pre- and post-operatively, 3 months after surgery, and at the final follow-up.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Characteristics</th><th align="left">DCMO-only</th><th align="left">DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin</th><th align="left"><italic>P</italic> value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Case number</td><td align="left">15 (16%)</td><td align="left">79 (84%)</td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="4">Preoperative</td></tr><tr><td align="left">IMA, degrees</td><td align="left">14.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.8</td><td align="left">14.8&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.5</td><td align="left">0.423</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HVA, degrees</td><td align="left">28.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;9.8</td><td align="left">33.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.5</td><td align="left">0.055</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="4">Postoperative</td></tr><tr><td align="left">IMA, degrees</td><td align="left">4.1&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.4</td><td align="left">3.6&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.3</td><td align="left">0.418</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HVA, degrees</td><td align="left">2.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.3</td><td align="left">&#x02212;0.3&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.2</td><td align="left">0.072</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="4">Postoperative 3 months</td></tr><tr><td align="left">IMA, degrees</td><td align="left">5.1&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.7</td><td align="left">3.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.6</td><td align="left">0.114</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HVA, degrees</td><td align="left">9.6&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.7</td><td align="left">1.8&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.9</td><td align="left">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="4">Final follow-up</td></tr><tr><td align="left">IMA, degrees</td><td align="left">4.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.0</td><td align="left">3.5&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.6</td><td align="left">0.120</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HVA, degrees</td><td align="left">9.8&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.3</td><td align="left">3.4&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.4</td><td align="left">0.002</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Recurrence</td><td align="left">5 (33.3%)</td><td align="left">6 (7.6%)</td><td align="left">0.014</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Data are presented as mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;standard deviation or number (%), unless otherwise indicated.</p><p>DCMO, distal chevron metatarsal osteotomy; HVA, hallux valgus angle; IMA, intermetatarsal angle; PDPAA, proximal-to-distal phalangeal articular angle.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par21">
<fig id="Fig4"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>HVA changes from preoperative to the final follow-up between the DCMO-only and DCMO&#x02009;+&#x02009;Akin groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_84273_Fig4_HTML" id="d33e635"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par22">As illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>, among patients in the DCMO-only group, those with recurrence had significantly higher preoperative PDPAA values than those without recurrence (7.6&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;5.9 vs. &#x02212;0.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.0 degrees; respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.013). Conversely, in patients who underwent both DCMO and Akin osteotomy, preoperative PDPAA was not associated with the recurrence (5.5&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;9.2 vs. 5.4&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.1 degrees; with and without recurrence, respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.984).</p><p id="Par23">
<fig id="Fig5"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p>The association between preoperative PDPAA on weightbearing radiograph and postoperative recurrence. Each box represents the interquartile range of values, with the bold horizontal lines within the boxes showing the median value.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2024_84273_Fig5_HTML" id="d33e656"/></fig>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec8"><title>Discussion</title><p id="Par24">This is the first study to evaluate the relationship between PDPAA and the pronation of the first phalanx in patients with hallux valgus. In our retrospective analysis, we found no significant difference in the PDPAA between the radiographs taken in the standard weightbearing position and those taken while correcting the pronation deformity by supination stress. Performing DCMO alone showed an overall higher recurrence rate compared to performing both DCMO and Akin osteotomy. Furthermore, a large preoperative PDPAA was associated with a postoperative recurrence when only DCMO was performed without Akin osteotomy.</p><p id="Par25">The HVIA is reported to increase after HV correction, often requiring an additional Akin osteotomy<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref></sup>. HVIA is underestimated due to the pronation deformity of the hallux, which can alter the position of the hallux phalanx in weightbearing AP radiographs. This pronation deformity may cause radiographs to resemble oblique or lateral views of the toe, hindering the accurate assessment of the HVIA. Consequently, determining the indication for Akin osteotomy based on preoperative HVIA is known to be challenging. Instead, Akin osteotomy is typically performed when a visible residual deformity remains after correcting the HV deformity<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref></sup>. Efforts have been made to establish reliable preoperative indications for Akin osteotomy to improve deformity correction planning. Kaufmann et al. reported that preoperative PDPAA greater than 8 degrees is associated with the loss of HVA correction<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>.</p><p id="Par26">We found that the PDPAA was not associated with the hallux pronation. Our goal was to explore whether the PDPAA is affected by rotational deformity of proximal phalanx. To investigate this, we acquired additional preoperative weightbearing radiographs while applying supination stress to the first toe, aligning it in a true AP position. The results showed no significant difference between the PDPAA measured on standard weightbearing radiographs and that corrected with supination force (5.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.0 vs. 4.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4 degrees, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.820). While HVIA, which reflects the alignment between the proximal and distal phalanges, is influenced by rotational deformity of the hallux, we hypothesized that the PDPAA would be less affected. This is because the PDPAA reflects the bony structure of the proximal phalanx&#x02014;that is, whether the proximal phalanx has a wedge shape oriented in the valgus direction, characterized by shorter lateral border and a longer medial border. Unlike HVIA, the characteristic of PDPAA being unaffected by hallux pronation holds clinical significance in that it could enable the accurate determination of the need for an Akin osteotomy based on standard standing radiographs taken preoperatively.</p><p id="Par27">We observed that a large preoperative PDPAA was associated with postoperative recurrence when the Akin osteotomy was not performed. Our findings align with a previous report by Kaufmann et al. which showed that a preoperative PDPAA greater than 8 degrees resulted in greater loss of correction when only DCMO was performed, without an Akin osteotomy (HVA at the final follow-up; 12.5&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.8 vs. 17.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.3 degrees; PDPAA&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;8 and &#x0003e;&#x02009;8 degrees, respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.001)<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>. They suggested considering an Akin osteotomy when the preoperative PDPAA is greater than 8 degrees<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>. Our results also showed a significantly higher recurrence rate when performing DCMO alone compared to when combined with an Akin osteotomy (33.3% vs. 7.6%, respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.014). Among patients who underwent only DCMO, recurrence group showed a larger preoperative PDPAA compared to non-recurrence group (7.6&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;5.9 vs. &#x02212;0.2&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.0 degrees; respectively, <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.013). Based on our observation, we believe that for patients with large PDPAA, additional Akin osteotomy should be considered to prevent postoperative recurrence. Unfortunately, due to the small number of recurrence cases, we were unable to establish a precise PDPAA cut-off value to recommend Akin osteotomy.</p><p id="Par28">Our study has some limitations. Firstly, the total number of patients was relatively small, making it impossible to determine the precise cut-off value of PDPAA for Akin osteotomy. Despite this, our results were consistent with previous report<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>, and given the limited number of studies evaluating the PDPAA, we believe that this study can contribute to the existing body of knowledge. Second, the distribution between groups was uneven. However, since the baseline characteristics were comparable between the two groups and the results were statistically significant, we believe that this limitation could be overcome. Thirdly, the follow-up period of our study cohort was relatively short (mean&#x02009;=&#x02009;12.9 months). Nonetheless, remarkable changes in HVA primarily occur during 3 months after surgery and stabilize thereafter<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref></sup>. Therefore, the results of our study might represent the results of longer follow-up periods. However, long-term studies are required for a more accurate conclusion. Lastly, we evaluated PDPAA using only plain radiographs and did not use weight-bearing CT. However, since there are very few studies conducted on PDPAA, we believe that this study presents meaningful results as preliminary research before evaluating PDPAA using weight-bearing CT.</p><p id="Par29">In conclusion, in patients with hallux valgus, the PDPAA was not significantly influenced by the hallux pronation. For patients with a large preoperative PDPAA, additional Akin osteotomy should be considered to prevent recurrence.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn><p><bold>Publisher&#x02019;s note</bold></p><p>Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></fn></fn-group><notes notes-type="author-contribution"><title>Author contributions</title><p>All authors contributed to the study conception and design. Material preparation, data collection and analysis were performed by Y.J.K, Y.R.C. The first draft of the manuscript was written by Y.J.K and Y.R.C and all authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript.</p></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Data availability</title><p>The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.</p></notes><notes><title>Declarations</title><notes id="FPar1" notes-type="COI-statement"><title>Competing interests</title><p id="Par30">The authors declare no competing interests.</p></notes></notes><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR1" publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dixon</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>LC</given-names></name><name><surname>Charlton</surname><given-names>TP</given-names></name><name><surname>Thordarson</surname><given-names>DB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Increased incide